<article xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Sci Rep</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Sci Rep</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Scientific Reports</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2045-2322</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group UK</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41233359</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12615697</article-id>
<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">23402</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41598-025-23402-3</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Evaluation of laser Doppler vibrometer&#x02019;s performance by a multimode laser and changeable reference arm length, equipped with an auto-focus system</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Seydipour</surname><given-names>Farshid</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Rezaei</surname><given-names>Fatemeh</given-names></name><address><email>fatemehrezaei@kntu.ac.ir</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1"/></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/0433abe34</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.411976.c</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 0369 2065</institution-id><institution>Department of Physics, </institution><institution>K. N. Toosi University of Technology, </institution></institution-wrap>Tehran, Iran </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>13</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>13</day><month>11</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><elocation-id>39831</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>30</day><month>5</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>7</day><month>10</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p><bold>Open Access</bold> This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="Abs1"><p id="Par1">In the present study, the theoretical framework underlying interferometric measurement using multimode lasers&#x02014;forming the basis of laser vibrometry&#x02014;is comprehensively described. A numerical simulation of the Doppler signal for the Laser Doppler Vibrometer (LDV) is conducted, and the technique for extracting vibration velocity via zero-crossing counting is detailed and subsequently verified through experimental assessment. In addition, two critical optical challenges in this field, namely the determination of the optimal object distances and laser focusing, are addressed. To overcome these issues, a novel set-up is proposed that incorporates systematic adjustments of the reference arm length along with active auto-focusing, facilitated by a laser rangefinder. Moreover, to validate the performance of the proposed device and facilitate comparison with conventional contact accelerometers, several frequency response function (FRF) tests were conducted under different conditions. The test cases included laser measurements when the accelerometer was not mounted on the plate, measurements obtained directly from the accelerometer, laser measurements when the beam was directed onto the accelerometer, and laser measurements when the beam was directed onto the plate. Comparative analysis of the data acquired via the accelerometer and the LDV confirmed the accuracy and reliability of the LDV set-up. Moreover, the measurements revealed that mass loading from the accelerometer caused detectable shifts in the natural frequencies of the 1-mm-thick galvanized plate&#x02014;shifts that typically require a correction step often treated as modal analysis noise in the literature. This underscores the advantage of LDV&#x02019;s non-contact nature in preserving the structure&#x02019;s true vibrational characteristics of the sample and eliminates the need for such corrective procedures.</p></abstract><kwd-group xml:lang="en"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Laser Doppler vibrometry</kwd><kwd>Multi-mode laser</kwd><kwd>Laser interferometry</kwd><kwd>Frequency response function</kwd><kwd>Modal test</kwd></kwd-group><kwd-group kwd-group-type="npg-subject"><title>Subject terms</title><kwd>Applied optics</kwd><kwd>Optical physics</kwd><kwd>Optical techniques</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Springer Nature Limited 2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="Sec1"><title>Introduction</title><p id="Par2">The Laser Doppler Vibrometer (LDV), recognized as a powerful tool in vibration measurement, offers numerous advantages over traditional methods. Unlike accelerometers, the LDV employs a non-contact technique that eliminates factors such as mass loading effects, allowing for precise measurements without altering the dynamic properties of the test object. These devices have extensive applications in fields such as aerospace<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref></sup>, automotive<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref></sup>, architecture<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref></sup>, and rail<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">13</xref></sup> for vibration testing and velocity measurement. Moreover, it is utilized in biology to measure eardrum<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref></sup> and heart displacements<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref></sup>; in acoustics for characterizing loudspeakers<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR22">22</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref></sup> and microphones<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref></sup>, as well as musical instruments such as violins<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref></sup> and guitars<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR27">27</xref></sup>; in the military for mine detection<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR28">28</xref></sup>; in security for remote surveillance<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref></sup> and in calibration for calibrating instruments such as accelerometers<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR32">32</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR33">33</xref></sup>. LDV operates by directing a laser beam onto a vibrating object and analyzing the reflected beam. According to the Doppler effect, the frequency of the reflected beam changes in proportion to the velocity of the moving object. By interfering this reflected beam with a reference beam and processing the resulting interference wave, the vibration characteristics of the object, such as velocity and displacement, can be accurately determined.</p><p id="Par3">Laser-Doppler techniques emerged shortly after the first laser demonstration in 1960<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR34">34</xref></sup>, leading to the development of Doppler anemometry in the 1960s<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR35">35</xref></sup>. LDV was introduced for vibrational analysis in the late 1960s<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR36">36</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR37">37</xref></sup>, followed by advancements in fiber-based and in-plane vibrometers during the 1980s<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR38">38</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR40">40</xref></sup>. By the 1990s, scanning LDV systems became commercially available for vibration analysis<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR42">42</xref></sup>, though multi-point LDV was needed for transient vibrations<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR43">43</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR47">47</xref></sup>. Recently, multi-point LDV capable of measuring multiple dimensions has been developed<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR48">48</xref></sup>, expanding applications across various fields<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR31">31</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR49">49</xref></sup>. Despite the numerous advantages of LDV, including its precision, measurement speed, frequency range, non-contact nature, and remote capability, certain challenges must be addressed for optimal measurement. These include the laser spot diameter on the target and the distance between the object and the device, both of which require optimization. For instance, Qu et al.<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR29">29</xref></sup> introduced an active autofocus system using a camera alongside an LDV device, employing triangulation-based distance measurement<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR50">50</xref></sup> to achieve fast focusing and an optimal laser spot diameter. However, one drawback of the triangulation method is its limited maximum measurable distance. In addition, Gatzwiller et al.<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR51">51</xref></sup> discussed challenges associated with LDV devices utilizing multimode lasers and proposes a solution where either the test object or the device is manually repositioned to achieve the optimal measurement distance. The root of this problem lies in the coherence length of multimode lasers, which is significantly shorter than that of single-mode lasers. Given LDV&#x02019;s widespread applications, manual repositioning is not a comprehensive or practical solution, as it can be cumbersome, time-consuming, or even infeasible in certain scenarios. Nevertheless, many commercial LDV systems currently available on the market, such as those produced by Polytec and Ometron, employ multimode lasers, primarily due to their relatively lower cost. Manufacturers of these devices typically recommend manual adjustment of the distance between the device and the object. However, this manual approach becomes particularly time-consuming and challenging in large-scale applications, such as vibration testing of automobile bodies, buildings, or bridges, where multiple points at varying distances must be scanned. In such cases, the vibrometer may need to be repositioned several times.</p><p id="Par4">In this study, a novel configuration is proposed in which the optical path difference between the reference and measurement arms is automatically adjusted according to the object distance. This is achieved by varying the reference arm length using a motorized mechanism, thereby ensuring the optimal path difference without requiring manual repositioning of either the object or the device. The proposed setup has been constructed as a compact, integrated system. To validate its performance, vibration and modal analysis tests were conducted on a galvanized plate in a vibration laboratory, and the results were compared with those obtained from a contact accelerometer. The strong agreement between the two sets of results confirms the accuracy of the proposed device and its potential applicability in industrial vibration measurement. The structure of this paper is as follows: first, a review of the mathematical principles underlying LDV is presented, with emphasis on laser spectral width, spectral profile, and mode structure. A simulation of the Doppler signal is then performed, from which the vibration velocity of the object is extracted. Two critical challenges in LDV, namely laser focusing and the optimal object-to-device distance, are discussed, and the proposed setup is introduced as a solution to these issues. Furthermore, vibration velocity is experimentally measured using the proposed configuration, and the device&#x02019;s performance is ultimately compared with that of a contact accelerometer in the frequency domain, both to verify its accuracy in vibration measurement and to highlight the inherent advantages of laser vibrometers over contact-based accelerometers.</p></sec><sec id="Sec2"><title>Materials and methods</title><sec id="Sec3"><title>LDV experimental set-up</title><p id="Par5">In laser Doppler vibrometers (LDVs), the incident laser beam on a moving object experiences a phase shift upon reflection, a phenomenon referred to as the Doppler effect. Since this phase shift is proportional to the object&#x02019;s velocity and vibration, it is possible to derive the object&#x02019;s velocity by applying a series of processing steps to the reflected beam. However, since the lasers employed in LDVs operate at frequencies around terahertz, directly detecting their phase shift with a photodetector alone is highly challenging. Optical interferometers such as Michelson and Mach&#x02013;Zehnder are employed to address this. In these interferometers, the general process involves splitting the output beam from a coherent laser source with frequency <inline-formula id="IEq1"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e306">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\nu$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq1.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> into two beams: a reference beam and a measurement beam. The measurement beam, after being reflected from the object, interferes with the reference beam at the detector. By analyzing the beat frequency generated by this interference, the phase variations of the reflected beam can be extracted.</p><p id="Par6">In this study, a Michelson interferometer was employed for measuring vibration velocity due to its simple set-up. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref> illustrates a typical schematic of a Michelson interferometer configuration, where the incident laser beam is divided into reference and measurement beams by a beam splitter. Typically, this beam splitter is a polarizing beam splitter (PBS), which, when combined with quarter-wave plates (QWPs) in the reference and measurement arms, prevents back-reflection into the laser and directs the signal toward the detector. Placing wave plates in front of the mirror and the object ensures that the polarization of the incident light rotates by 90 degrees after reflection and passing through the plates again. As a result, the interfering beams at the detector possess orthogonal polarizations. To facilitate interference, a polarizer set at angle of about 45 degrees to the polarization of the two interfering beams is positioned in front of the detector.<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Optical set-up of the Michelson interferometer.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig1_HTML" id="MO1"/></fig></p><p id="Par7">In this research, a 1&#x000a0;mW multimode helium&#x02013;neon laser with a wavelength of 632.8&#x000a0;nm and a mode spacing of 730&#x000a0;MHz was employed in a Michelson interferometry set-up for vibrometry of a galvanized sheet measuring 20&#x02009;&#x000d7;&#x02009;20&#x000a0;cm with a thickness of 1&#x000a0;mm. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref> presents the measurement set-up image, including the LDV image, the exciter model 4809 from B&#x00026;K, and the galvanized sheet mounted on the exciter shaft.<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Measurement set-up including the LDV, exciter, and galvanized plate.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig2_HTML" id="MO2"/></fig></p><p id="Par8">Various methods exist for extracting velocity, which can be classified into analog and digital approaches. In analog methods, electronic hardware components such as frequency-to-voltage converters, phase-locked loops, and fringe counters are utilized. In digital methods, the signal is initially converted into digital data using an analog-to-digital converter (ADC). This data is then sent to a computer or processing board, where algorithms are employed to extract velocity. Among the digital velocity extraction methods, the inverse tangent method and the fringe-counting method are notable<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR52">52</xref></sup>. The inverse tangent method can be utilized in both homodyne and heterodyne set-ups, while the fringe-counting method is applicable to the configuration employed in this study. In this method, velocity or displacement is determined by counting the zero crossings of the raw signal.</p></sec><sec id="Sec4"><title>Multi-mode lasers in LDV experiment</title><p id="Par9">In LDV systems, the multi-mode helium&#x02013;neon lasers are commonly used. However, the coherence length of these lasers (typically less than 20&#x000a0;cm) is significantly shorter than that of single-mode lasers, which can extend to several hundred meters. Generally, multi-mode lasers are preferred in commercial systems due to their lower cost and higher power output. The primary challenge arises from the difference in the optical path lengths traveled by the reference and measurement beams, which should not be large enough to reduce the interference signal strength significantly. This paper investigates the theory and mathematics of interferometry using both single-mode and multi-mode lasers, considering the laser&#x02019;s frequency bandwidth, and presents numerical simulations of the interference signal in the vibration condition of the moving object, along with the method for extracting vibration velocity from it. To address this challenge, a Michelson interferometer-based set-up is proposed, where the reference mirror is adjustable over the length of the laser cavity, and a laser rangefinder is employed. The method operates as follows: the distance to the object is first measured using the rangefinder, and according to this distance, the reference mirror is displaced by a specific amount, ensuring the maximum signal is achieved at that particular distance. Additionally, with the use of the laser rangefinder and an optical system with an adjustable lens, the set-up is equipped with an active autofocus system that can be employed to enhance the received signal magnitude. To validate the device&#x02019;s performance and compare it with contact accelerometers, multiple stages of FRF testing were conducted under varying conditions. The test cases included laser measurements when the accelerometer was not mounted on the plate, measurements obtained directly from the accelerometer, laser measurements when the beam was directed onto the accelerometer, and laser measurements when the beam was directed onto the plate.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec5"><title>Governing theoretical equations</title><p id="Par10">In LDV, the reference and measurement beams can be expressed using the following equations<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ1"><label>1</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e357">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$E_{r} \left( t \right) = E_{{0r}} cos\left( {2\pi \nu _{r} t + \phi _{{0r}} } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ1.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ2"><label>2</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e363">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$E_{m} \left( t \right) = E_{0m} cos\left( { 2\pi \nu_{m} t + \phi_{0m} + \phi_{m} \left( t \right)} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ2.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par11">In these equations, <inline-formula id="IEq2"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e371">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{E}}_{{\text{r}}} \left( {\text{t}} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq2.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq3"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e377">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{E}}_{{\text{m}}} \left( {\text{t}} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq3.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represent the electric fields, while <inline-formula id="IEq4"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e383">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{E}}_{{0{\text{r}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq4.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq5"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e389">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{E}}_{{0{\text{m}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq5.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> denote the field amplitudes. The initial phases of the reference and measurement waves are represented by <inline-formula id="IEq6"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e395">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upphi _{{0{\text{r}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq6.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq7"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e402">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upphi _{{0{\text{m}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq7.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, respectively. While <inline-formula id="IEq8"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e408">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upphi _{{0{\text{m}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq7.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> corresponds to the equilibrium state of vibration and remains constant over time, <inline-formula id="IEq9"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e414">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upphi _{{\text{m}}} \left( {\text{t}} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq9.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> indicates the phase variation of the measurement wave relative to the equilibrium state. This variation, caused by the object&#x02019;s vibrations, is time-dependent. In some configurations, a frequency shift <inline-formula id="IEq10"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e420">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{b}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq10.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, usually introduced by an acousto-optic modulator, is applied to either the reference or measurement arm. This shift allows for the measurement of vibration direction. Consequently, in these setups, referred to as heterodyne configurations, the reference beam frequency (<inline-formula id="IEq11"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e426">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{r}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq11.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) and the measurement beam frequency (<inline-formula id="IEq12"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e432">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{m}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq12.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) are not equal, according to the relation <inline-formula id="IEq13"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e439">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{m}}} = {\upnu }_{{\text{r}}} + {\upnu }_{{\text{b}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq13.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p><p id="Par12">However, in set-ups where no frequency shift is applied&#x02014;such as the one considered in this study&#x02014;the relation <inline-formula id="IEq14"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e447">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{\text{m}}={\upnu }_{\text{r}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq14.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> holds. As a result, the intensity of the beam produced by the interference of these two waves is described by the Eq. (<xref rid="Equ3" ref-type="disp-formula">3</xref>) <sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ3"><label>3</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e460">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} I\left( t \right) &#x00026; = I_{r} + I_{m} + 2\sqrt {I_{r} I_{m} } cos\left( {\phi \left( t \right)} \right) \\ \phi \left( t \right) &#x00026; = \phi_{02} + \phi_{2} \left( t \right) - \phi_{01} ,\phi_{2} \left( t \right) = 2ks\left( t \right) = 4\pi s\left( t \right)/\lambda \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ3.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par13">In this equation, <inline-formula id="IEq15"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e468">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}}\left( {\text{t}} \right){ }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq15.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represents the displacement of the vibrating object relative to its equilibrium position. If the other phase components remain constant, it can be stated that <inline-formula id="IEq16"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e474">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upphi _{2} \left( {\text{t}} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq16.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the phase component responsible for generating bright and dark fringes or, equivalently, for modulating the amplitude of the detector&#x02019;s output signal. In LDV, the goal is to ascertain the frequency of these variations, commonly known as the Doppler frequency (<inline-formula id="IEq17"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e480">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{d}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq17.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>). This objective can be accomplished due to the simple relation between the time derivative of the phase and frequency as below<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ4"><label>4</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e490">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\nu_{d} = d\phi \left( t \right)/2\pi dt = d\phi_{2} \left( t \right)/2\pi dt = 2ds\left( t \right)/\lambda dt = 2V/\lambda$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ4.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par14">In this equation, it is utilized that the vibration velocity (V) is the time derivative of displacement (V&#x02009;=&#x02009;ds(t)/dt). Finally, the Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ5" ref-type="disp-formula">5</xref>) can be derived for the vibration velocity<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ5"><label>5</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e505">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V = \lambda \nu_{d} /2$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ5.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><sec id="Sec6"><title>Velocity calculation</title><p id="Par15">For a sinusoidal vibration, the displacement can be expressed as<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR41">41</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ6"><label>6</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e519">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$s\left( t \right) = A {\text{sin}}\left( {2\pi \nu_{vib} t} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ6.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula>where, <inline-formula id="IEq18"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e526">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{A}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq18.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represents the vibration amplitude, and <inline-formula id="IEq19"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e532">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{{\text{vib}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq19.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> denotes the vibration frequency of the object.</p><p id="Par16">To calculate the vibration velocity from this signal, the zero-crossing method is employed. The process involves the following steps:</p><p id="Par17">First, the zero-crossings, which correspond to the points where the signal intersects the x-axis, are identified. If the time interval between two consecutive zero crossings, equivalent to half a wavelength, is denoted as <inline-formula id="IEq20"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e542">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{t}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq20.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, the instantaneous frequency is expressed by <inline-formula id="IEq21"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e548">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{d}}} = 1/2{\text{t }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq21.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. By substituting <inline-formula id="IEq22"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e554">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{\text{d}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq17.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> into Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ5" ref-type="disp-formula">5</xref>), the velocity <inline-formula id="IEq23"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e563">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq23.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> at any given moment can be determined.</p><p id="Par18">Once all zero crossings are identified, their occurrence times are sequentially stored in an array.<disp-formula id="Equ7"><label>7</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e571">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$L = [t_{k} ,t_{k + 1,} ,t_{k + 2} , \ldots ] , k = 0,1,2, \ldots$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ7.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par19">In this array, <inline-formula id="IEq24"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e579">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{t}}_{{{\text{k}},}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq24.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represents the times at which zero crossings occur. Assuming that the velocity remains constant between two consecutive zero crossings, the object&#x02019;s velocity during the time interval between two successive zero crossings is derived from the following equation:<disp-formula id="Equ8"><label>8</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e585">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V_{k} = \lambda /4\left( {t_{k + 1} - t_{k} } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ8.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p></sec><sec id="Sec7"><title>Simulation of visibility as a function of path difference and laser frequency bandwidth</title><p id="Par20">If in Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ3" ref-type="disp-formula">3</xref>), the intensities of the reference and measurement beams are assumed to be equal (<inline-formula id="IEq25"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e598">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$i.e. {\text{I}}_{{\text{r}}} { } = {\text{ I}}_{{\text{m}}} { } = {\text{ I}}_{0}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq25.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>), then the following equation is derived under the assumption of single-frequency beams:<disp-formula id="Equ9"><label>9</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e604">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I = 2 I_{0} + 2I_{0} cos\left( {\phi \left( {\nu ,\tau } \right)} \right) = 2I_{0} \left[ {1 + \cos \left( {\phi \left( {\nu ,\tau } \right)} \right)} \right]$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ9.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par21">If, instead of a monochromatic beam, a frequency-distributed beam centered around a frequency <inline-formula id="IEq26"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e612">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{0{ }}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq26.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is utilized, which reflects reality, then it can be considered as:<disp-formula id="Equ10"><label>10</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e618">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\nu = \nu_{0} - \nu^{\prime}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ10.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula>where, <inline-formula id="IEq27"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e625">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\upnu {\prime}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq27.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represents the frequency difference from <inline-formula id="IEq28"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e631">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{0}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq28.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>.</p><p id="Par22">Therefore, using Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ10" ref-type="disp-formula">10</xref>), it can be written as:<disp-formula id="Equ11"><label>11</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e642">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\phi \left( {\nu ,s} \right) = 2\pi \nu \tau = 2\pi \left( {\nu_{0} - \nu^{\prime}} \right)\tau = \phi \left( {\nu^{\prime},\tau } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ11.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par23">Moreover, Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ9" ref-type="disp-formula">9</xref>( can be rewritten in a form that <inline-formula id="IEq29"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e653">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{I}}_{0}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq29.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is frequency-dependent<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ12"><label>12</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e666">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I = 2I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right) \left[ {1 + \cos \left( {\phi \left( {\nu^{\prime},\tau } \right)} \right)} \right]$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ12.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par24">To calculate the interference signal intensity across all frequencies, the equation above must be integrated as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ13"><label>13</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e674">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right) [1 + \cos \left( {\phi \left( {\nu^{\prime},\tau } \right)} \right)]d\nu^{\prime}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ13.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par25">By utilizing Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ10" ref-type="disp-formula">10</xref>), the signal intensity can be written as:<disp-formula id="Equ14"><label>14</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e685">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right) \left[ {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \left( {\nu_{0} - \nu^{\prime}} \right)\tau } \right)} \right]d\nu^{\prime}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ14.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par26">Furthermore, with the assistance of the following trigonometric relation:<disp-formula id="Equ15"><label>15</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e693">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\cos \left( {a - b} \right) = \cos \left( a \right)\cos \left( b \right) + \sin \left( a \right)sin\left( b \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ15.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par27">The Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ14" ref-type="disp-formula">14</xref>) can be written as follows<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ16"><label>16</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e711">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I = P + C\cos (2\pi \nu_{0} \tau ) - S\sin \left( {2\pi \nu_{0} \tau } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ16.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula>where,<disp-formula id="Equ17"><label>17</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e718">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{gathered} P = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)d\nu^{\prime} = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)exp\left( { - i2\pi \nu ^{\prime}*0} \right)d\nu^{\prime} = 2\;{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( {\tau = 0} \right) = const \hfill \\ C = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)\cos \left( {2\pi \nu{\prime} \tau } \right)d\Omega = 2\;Re\left( {\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)exp\left( { - i2\pi \nu ^{\prime} \tau } \right)d\nu^{\prime}} \right) = 2\;Re\left( {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right) \hfill \\ S = 2\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)\sin \left( {2\pi \nu^{\prime}\tau } \right)d\nu^{\prime} = - 2\; Im\left( {\smallint I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)exp\left( { - i2\pi \nu ^{\prime} \tau } \right)d\nu^{\prime}} \right) = - 2\;Im\left( {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right) \hfill \\ \end{gathered}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ17.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula>here, <inline-formula id="IEq30"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e725">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}\left[ {{\text{I}}_{0} \left( {{\upnu ^{\prime}}} \right)} \right]\left( {\uptau } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq30.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the Fourier transform of the laser&#x02019;s frequency spectrum, which may sometimes be written in a simplified form of <inline-formula id="IEq31"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e732">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}\left[ {{\text{I}}_{0} } \right]{ }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq31.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> or <inline-formula id="IEq32"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e738">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}\left[ {{\text{I}}_{0} } \right]\left( {\uptau } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq32.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) for convenience. The equation for the fringe interference visibility, first introduced by Michelson, is expressed as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ18"><label>18</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e744">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V = \left( {I_{max} - I_{min} } \right)/\left( {I_{max} + I_{min} } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ18.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par28">Based on this equation, it is understood that to plot the visibility, it is essential to determine the extreme values of <inline-formula id="IEq33"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e752">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{I}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq33.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> in Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ16" ref-type="disp-formula">16</xref>) as a function of <inline-formula id="IEq34"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e761">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{S}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq34.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, which implies that it is needed to set it as<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ19"><label>19</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e774">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} dI/ds &#x00026; = d/ds \left[ {P + C\cos \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right)) - S\sin \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right)} \right] \\ &#x00026; = - \omega_{0} \left[ {C\sin \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right) + S\cos \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right)} \right] = 0 \to C\sin \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right) \\ &#x00026; = - S\cos \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right) \to sin\left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right)/cos\left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right) = \tan \left( {2\pi v_{0} \tau } \right) \\ &#x00026; = - S/C \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ19.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par29">By substituting into the Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ16" ref-type="disp-formula">16</xref>), the maximum and minimum intensity magnitudes will be obtained as follows<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ20"><label>20</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e792">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} I_{max} &#x00026; = P + \sqrt {S^{2} + C^{2} } \\ I_{min} &#x00026; = P - \sqrt {S^{2} + C^{2} } \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ20.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par30">By substituting the Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ20" ref-type="disp-formula">20</xref>) into Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ18" ref-type="disp-formula">18</xref>), the following equation for visibility is obtained:<disp-formula id="Equ21"><label>21</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e807">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V = \sqrt {S^{2} + C^{2} } /P$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ21.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par31">From the above equations, it is evident that the value of <inline-formula id="IEq35"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e815">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{P}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq35.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is independent of <inline-formula id="IEq36"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e821">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\uptau$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq36.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq37"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e827">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\text{s}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq37.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. Using Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ17" ref-type="disp-formula">17</xref>), the term <inline-formula id="IEq38"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e836">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\sqrt{{\text{S}}^{2}+{\text{C}}^{2}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq38.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> can be rewritten as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ22"><label>22</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e843">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\sqrt {S^{2} + C^{2} } = 2\sqrt { Re\left( {{ \mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]} \right) ^{2} + Im\left( {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]} \right)^{2} } = 2\left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]} \right|$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ22.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par32">Therefore, Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ21" ref-type="disp-formula">21</xref>) can be rewritten as below<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR53">53</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR54">54</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ23"><label>23</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e861">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V = \sqrt {S^{2} + C^{2} } /P = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/{}\left( {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]\left( 0 \right)} \right) = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/const \to V \propto \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0} } \right]} \right|$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ23.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par33">By analyzing this equation, it can be concluded that calculating P is only necessary if the goal is to determine the exact value of visibility; however, for relative visibility values, this calculation is not required.</p><p id="Par34">The Gaussian <inline-formula id="IEq39"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e871">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{I}}_{{0,{\text{g}}}} \left( {\upnu ^{\prime } } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq39.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and Lorentzian <inline-formula id="IEq40"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e877">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{I}}_{{0,{\text{l}}}} \left( {\upnu } \right){ }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq40.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> distributions of intensity can be calculated as<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ24"><label>24</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e887">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} &#x00026; I_{{0,g}} \left( \nu \right) = I_{{\nu 0}} exp\left( { - 4ln2\left( {\left( {\nu - \nu _{0} } \right)/\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} } \right)} \right)^{2} } \right) \\ &#x00026; = I_{{\nu 0}} exp\left( { - 4ln2\left( {\nu \prime /\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} } \right)} \right)^{2} ~} \right) = I_{{\nu 0}} exp\left( { - ~\alpha v^{{\prime 2}} ~} \right) = I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right) \\ &#x00026; \alpha = 4ln2/\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}}^{2} ~} \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ24.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ25"><label>25</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e893">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} I_{{0,l}} \left( \nu \right) &#x00026; = I_{{\nu 0}} \left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)/\left( {\left( {\nu - \nu _{0} } \right)^{2} + \left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)^{2} } \right)} \right) \\ &#x00026; = I_{{\nu 0}} ~\left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {~\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)/\left( {\nu ^{{\prime 2}} + \left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2~} \right)^{2} } \right)} \right) = I_{{0,l}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ25.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par35">The visibility equation for the Gaussian distribution <inline-formula id="IEq41"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e901">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}_{{\text{g}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq41.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, and the Lorentzian distribution <inline-formula id="IEq42"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e907">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}_{{\text{l}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq42.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> can be obtained as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ26"><label>26</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e913">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} V_{g} &#x00026; = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right)} \right]\left( 0 \right) = I_{{\nu 0}} \;\sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right))/\left( {I_{{\nu 0}} \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } } \right) \\ &#x00026; = exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right),\quad \tau \ge 0 \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ26.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ27"><label>27</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e919">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} V_{l} &#x00026; = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,l}} ~\left( \nu \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,l}} ~\left( {\nu ^{\prime}~} \right)} \right]\left( 0 \right) = I_{{\nu 0}} exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} ~\tau } \right))/I_{{\nu 0}} \\ &#x00026; = exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} ~\tau } \right),\quad \tau \ge 0~ \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ27.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula>where, <inline-formula id="IEq43"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e926">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq43.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> can be written as<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR56">56</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR57">57</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ28"><label>28</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e940">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}\left[ {e^{{ - \alpha x^{2} }} } \right] = \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - i2\pi x_{0} \zeta } \right)exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \zeta } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ28.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ29"><label>29</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e946">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathcal{F}}\left[ { \left( {1/\pi } \right) \left( {\Gamma /2} \right)/\left( {\left( {x - x_{0} } \right)^{2} + \left( {\Gamma /2} \right)^{2} } \right)} \right]\left( k \right) = exp\left( { - 2\pi ikx_{0} } \right) exp\left( { - \Gamma \pi \left| k \right|} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ29.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par36">Generally, the coherence length is calculated by <inline-formula id="IEq44"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e954">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{\text{c}}} = {\text{c*}}\uptau _{{\text{c}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq44.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula><sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref></sup> which is directly related to the coherence time <inline-formula id="IEq45"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e963">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\uptau _{{\text{c}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq45.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. Since the derived visibility equations for the single-mode laser are Gaussian, and visibility does not reach zero at any finite time <inline-formula id="IEq46"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e969">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\tau }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq46.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is typically expressed as the Full Width at Half Maximum (FWHM) or the 1/e visibility. In the laboratory, what is directly measured is <inline-formula id="IEq47"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e975">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\Delta \nu }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq47.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, not <inline-formula id="IEq48"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e981">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq48.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>; therefore, it is needed to establish a relation between <inline-formula id="IEq49"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e988">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\Delta \nu }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq47.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq50"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e994">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq48.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. To achieve this purpose, <inline-formula id="IEq51"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1000">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
