				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\uptau _{{{\text{c}}_{{{\text{FWHM}}}} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq52.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq53"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1012">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\uptau _{{{\text{c}}_{{1/{\text{e}}}} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq53.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and then, following the approach outlined in Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ30" ref-type="disp-formula">30</xref>), these two parameters can be extracted accordingly. Note that since Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ26" ref-type="disp-formula">26</xref>) and (<xref rid="Equ27" ref-type="disp-formula">27</xref>) are true for <inline-formula id="IEq54"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1028">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau } \ge 0{ }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq54.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> , a complete Gaussian or Lorentzian curve can&#x02019;t be attained. Instead, half-Gaussian and half-Lorentzian curves for visibility are employed. Therefore, for example, to calculate the FWHM, <inline-formula id="IEq55"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1034">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{{{\text{FWHM}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq55.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is defined as the distance from <inline-formula id="IEq56"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1040">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau } = 0{ }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq56.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> to the point where <inline-formula id="IEq57"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1046">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq23.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is equal to 0.5.<disp-formula id="Equ30"><label>30</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1053">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} V_{g} = exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right) &#x00026; \to \tau_{{c_{FWHM,g} }} = \sqrt {\alpha ln2} /\pi = \left( {2ln2/\pi } \right)\left( {1/\Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right) \\ &#x00026; \to \tau_{{c_{1/e,g} }} = \surd \alpha /\pi = \left( {2\sqrt {ln2} } \right)/\pi )\left( {1/\Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right) \\ V_{l} = exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} \left| \tau \right|} \right) &#x00026; \to \tau_{{c_{FWHM,l} }} = \left( {ln2/\pi } \right) \left( {1/\left( {\Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right)} \right) \\ &#x00026; \to \tau_{{c_{1/e,g} }} = 1/\left( {\pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ30.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par37">Finally, for the coherence length, the following equation can be considered<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ31"><label>31</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1065">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} &#x00026; L_{{c_{{FWHM,g,}} }} = c\tau _{{c_{{FWHM,g}} }} = \left( {2ln2/\pi } \right)~\left( {~c/\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} ~} \right) \\ &#x00026; L_{{c_{{1/e,g}} }} = c~\tau _{{c_{{1/e,g}} }} = \left( {2\sqrt {ln2} /\pi } \right)\left( {c/\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} } \right) \\ &#x00026; L_{{c_{{FWHM,l}} }} = c~\tau _{{c_{{FWHM,l}} }} = \left( {ln2/\pi } \right)\left( {c/\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} ~} \right) \\ &#x00026; L_{{c_{{1/e,l}} }} = c~\tau _{{c_{{1/e,g}} }} = \left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {c/\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} ~} \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ31.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par38">To plot the V magnitude versus <inline-formula id="IEq58"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1073">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\Delta \nu }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq47.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> quantity, it is necessary to substitute &#x003c4;&#x02009;=&#x02009;s/c, and <inline-formula id="IEq59"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1079">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upalpha } = 4{\text{ln}}2/{{\Delta \nu }}_{{{\text{FWHM}}}}^{2} { }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq59.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> into Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ30" ref-type="disp-formula">30</xref>) for visibility as bellows:<disp-formula id="Equ32"><label>32</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1088">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} V_{g} &#x00026; = exp\left( { - \left( {\pi s\Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right)^{2} /\left( {4c^{2} ln2 } \right)} \right) \\ V_{l} &#x00026; = exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} \tau } \right) = exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} s/c } \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ32.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par39">The simulation for a multi-mode laser is now represented. Assuming the use of a multi-mode laser with a longitudinal mode spacing frequency equal to <inline-formula id="IEq60"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1096">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{{\text{lsm}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq60.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and a longitudinal mode number of 3, the following equations for the Gaussian and Lorentzian spectra can be utilized:<disp-formula id="Equ33"><label>33</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1102">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right) &#x00026; = \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)exp\left( { - \alpha \left( {\nu ^{\prime } - \nu _{{lsm}} } \right)^{2} } \right) + I_{{\nu 0}} exp\left( { - \alpha \nu ^{{^{\prime } 2}} } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad + \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)exp\left( { - \alpha \left( {\nu ^{\prime } + \nu _{{lsm}} } \right)^{2} } \right) \\ I_{{0,l}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right) &#x00026; = \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)\left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)/\left( {\left( {\nu ^{\prime } - \nu _{{lsm}} } \right)^{2} + \left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)^{2} } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad + I_{{\nu 0}} \left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)/\left( {\nu ^{{\prime 2}} + \left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)^{2} } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad + \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)\left( {1/\pi } \right)\left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)/\left( {\left( {\nu ^{\prime } + \nu _{{lsm}} } \right)^{2} + \left( {\Delta \nu _{{FWHM}} /2} \right)^{2} } \right) \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ33.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par40">To calculate the visibility, the Fourier transform of the Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ33" ref-type="disp-formula">33</xref>) is required, which is obtained using Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ28" ref-type="disp-formula">28</xref>) and (<xref rid="Equ29" ref-type="disp-formula">29</xref>) as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ34"><label>34</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1119">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} {\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right) &#x00026; = \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)\sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - i2\pi \nu _{{lms}} \tau } \right)exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad + I_{{\nu 0}} \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad + \left( {I_{{\nu 0}} /2} \right)\sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - i2\pi \left( { - \nu _{{lms}} } \right)\tau } \right)exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right) \\ &#x00026; \quad = I_{{\nu 0}} \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp( - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} \\ &#x00026; /\alpha )\left( {1 + (exp\left( { - i2\pi \nu _{{lms}} \tau } \right) + exp\left( {i2\pi \nu _{{lms}} \tau } \right)/2} \right) \\ &#x00026; = I_{{\nu 0}} \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right)\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \nu _{{lms}} \tau } \right)} \right) \\ {\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{{0,g}} \left( {\nu ^{\prime } } \right)} \right]\left( 0 \right) &#x00026; = 2I_{{\nu 0}} \sqrt {\pi /\alpha } \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ34.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par41">Thus, for visibility, the expression can be calculated as below:<disp-formula id="Equ35"><label>35</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1128">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} V_{g} &#x00026; = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0,g} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0,g} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( 0 \right) \\ &#x00026; = \left( {1/2} \right) exp\left( { - \left( {\pi \tau } \right)^{2} /\alpha } \right)\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \nu_{lms} \tau } \right) } \right),\quad \tau \ge 0 \\ V_{l} &#x00026; = \left| {{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0,l} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( \tau \right)} \right|/{\mathcal{F}}\left[ {I_{0,l} \left( {\nu^{\prime}} \right)} \right]\left( 0 \right) \\ &#x00026; = \left( {1/2} \right) exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} \tau } \right)\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \nu_{lms} \tau } \right)} \right),\quad \tau \ge 0 \\ \end{aligned}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ35.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par42">In the single-mode case, it was explained that the reason for calculating <inline-formula id="IEq61"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1136">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{{{\text{FWHM}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq55.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> or <inline-formula id="IEq62"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1142">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{{1/{\text{e}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq62.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> was that the visibility would not reach zero except at infinity. However, for multi-mode lasers, it is known that due to the periodic term of <inline-formula id="IEq63"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1148">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2{{\uppi \nu }}_{{{\text{lms}}}} {\uptau }} \right)} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq63.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, the visibility periodically drops to zero. Moreover, since calculating <inline-formula id="IEq64"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1154">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{{{\text{FWHM}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq55.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> or <inline-formula id="IEq65"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1160">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{{1/{\text{e}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq62.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, in this case, analytically is challenging, the numerical calculation is presented here. However, for the multi-mode case, <inline-formula id="IEq66"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1167">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }_{0}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq66.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is defined as the shortest time interval between the reference and measurement beams that results in zero visibility. It is obtained by zeroing the periodic term within the parentheses in the two equations above and is independent of the spectral profile. Its value is the same for both Gaussian and Lorentzian profiles.<disp-formula id="Equ36"><label>36</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1173">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \nu_{lms} \tau_{0} } \right)} \right) = 0 \to \tau_{0} = 1/2\nu_{lms}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ36.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par43">Therefore, <inline-formula id="IEq67"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1181">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{c}}_{0} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq67.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, the shortest path difference between the reference and measurement beams, leads to the visibility reaching zero and is derived from the following relation:<disp-formula id="Equ37"><label>37</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1187">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$L_{{c_{0} }} = c \tau_{0} = c/2\nu_{lms}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ37.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par44">Additionally, for the multi-mode case, a parameter known as the periodical coherence length <inline-formula id="IEq68"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1195">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{pc}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq68.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, where <inline-formula id="IEq69"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1201">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{pc}},{\text{FWHM}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq69.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq70"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1207">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{pc}},1/{\text{e}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq70.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> represent the maximum path differences that can decrease the visibility to 0.5 and 1/e of its maximum value, respectively. Their values are derived from Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ31" ref-type="disp-formula">31</xref>).</p><p id="Par45">It is recognized that the following relation exists between the longitudinal mode spacing and the laser cavity length<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR55">55</xref></sup>:<disp-formula id="Equ38"><label>38</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1222">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\nu_{lms} = c/\left( {2L_{cavity} } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ38.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par46">By substituting (38) into (37), the following equation can be derived:<disp-formula id="Equ39"><label>39</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1230">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$L_{{c_{0} }} = L_{cavity}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ39.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par47">From the two recent equations, it can be concluded that for a multi-mode laser with <inline-formula id="IEq71"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1238">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upnu }_{{{\text{lms}}}} = 730{\text{ MHz}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq71.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, the laser cavity length and <inline-formula id="IEq72"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1244">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{c}}_{0} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq67.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> will equal 20.5 cm.</p><p id="Par48">By substituting <inline-formula id="IEq73"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1252">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\uptau }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq48.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq74"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1258">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\upalpha }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq74.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> into the Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ35" ref-type="disp-formula">35</xref>), the following equations are obtained as:<disp-formula id="Equ40"><label>40</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1267">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V_{g} = \left( {1/2} \right) exp\left( { - \left( {\pi s\Delta \nu_{FWHM} } \right)^{2} /\left( {4c^{2} ln2 } \right)} \right)\left( {1 + cos\left( {2\pi \nu \_lms s/c} \right)} \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ40.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ41"><label>41</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1273">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V_{l} = \left( {1/2} \right)exp\left( { - \pi \Delta \nu_{FWHM} s/c} \right)\left( {1 + \cos \left( {2\pi \nu_{lms} s/c} \right) } \right)$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ41.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec8"><title>Results and discussion on experimental and theoretical results</title><sec id="Sec9"><title>Theoretical results</title><p id="Par49">Based on the Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ9" ref-type="disp-formula">9</xref>), it can be theoretically inferred that by assuming the beam is monochromatic, the maximum and minimum intensities of the beam are calculated by:<disp-formula id="Equ42"><label>42</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1288">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$I_{max} = 4I_{o} ,I_{min} = 0$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ42.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par50">As a result, in this ideal case, visibility is equal to 1. Therefore, if visibility is plotted as a function of path difference, Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref> is obtained.<fig id="Fig3"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Visibility versus path difference for a single-frequency beam.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig3_HTML" id="MO3"/></fig></p><p id="Par51">From Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ42" ref-type="disp-formula">42</xref>) and Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>, it is evident that for a monochromatic beam, the visibility is independent of the path difference.</p><p id="Par52">If the laser has a frequency bandwidth, by employing Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ32" ref-type="disp-formula">32</xref>), the variations of V magnitude versus <inline-formula id="IEq75"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1319">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\Delta \nu }}_{{{\text{FWHM}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq75.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> can be shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4</xref> for a path difference of s&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.3&#x000a0;m.<fig id="Fig4"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>Evolution of visibility versus frequency width at s&#x02009;=&#x02009;30&#x000a0;cm, for both Gaussian and Lorentzian distributions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig4_HTML" id="MO4"/></fig></p><p id="Par53">By analyzing this graph, it can be concluded that for a fixed path difference, as the laser&#x02019;s frequency width increases, the visibility decreases. It is also worth noting that for narrow frequency widths, the visibility of the Gaussian distribution exceeds that of the Lorentzian distribution, whereas at larger frequency widths, the two distributions tend to converge towards equality. Additionally, using Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ32" ref-type="disp-formula">32</xref>), <inline-formula id="IEq76"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1340">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq23.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> distribution as a function of s can be plotted for <inline-formula id="IEq77"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1346">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{\Delta \nu }}_{{{\text{FWHM}}}} = 1{\text{ MHz}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq77.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, for both Gaussian and Lorentzian spectra as shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig5" ref-type="fig">5</xref>.<fig id="Fig5"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p>Visibility versus path difference for a single-mode laser with a frequency width of <inline-formula id="IEq78"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1361">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta \nu = 1 MHz$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq78.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> for both Gaussian (blue) and Lorentzian (orange) spectral line profiles.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig5_HTML" id="MO5"/></fig></p><p id="Par54">Using Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ40" ref-type="disp-formula">40</xref>) and (<xref rid="Equ44" ref-type="disp-formula">44</xref>), the variation of visibility versus path difference for a multi-mode laser can be illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig6" ref-type="fig">6</xref> for the Gaussian spectrum, while the Lorentzian spectrum is depicted in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig7" ref-type="fig">7</xref>. As observed in these figures, visibility exhibits an exponential-periodic pattern, oscillating between 0 and a value less than 1. Specifically, it reaches zero at odd multiples of the laser cavity length (<inline-formula id="IEq79"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1383">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{cavity}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq79.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) and peaks at even multiples. At the path differences of <inline-formula id="IEq80"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1390">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{cp}}_{{{\text{FWHM}}}} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq80.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq81"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1396">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{cp}}_{{1/{\text{e}}}} }}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq81.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, visibility drops to 0.5 and <inline-formula id="IEq82"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1402">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\frac{1}{{\text{e}}},$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq82.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> respectively (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig6" ref-type="fig">6</xref>b and <xref rid="Fig7" ref-type="fig">7</xref>b). As shown in these figures (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig6" ref-type="fig">6</xref>a and <xref rid="Fig7" ref-type="fig">7</xref>a), the peak visibility for <inline-formula id="IEq83"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1421">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}} = 0$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq83.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is 1, but it decreases exponentially for larger values of <inline-formula id="IEq84"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1427">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq84.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>.<fig id="Fig6"><label>Fig. 6</label><caption><p>Visibility versus path difference for a multi-mode laser, where the modes exhibit a Gaussian spectrum with a width of 1&#x000a0;MHz, and the frequency spacing between modes is 730&#x000a0;MHz. (<bold>a</bold>) indicates that the path difference ranges from 0 to 500&#x000a0;m, while (<bold>b</bold>) demonstrates that the path difference varies from 0 to 1&#x000a0;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig6_HTML" id="MO6"/></fig><fig id="Fig7"><label>Fig. 7</label><caption><p>Visibility versus path difference for a multi-mode laser, where the modes show a Lorentzian spectrum with a width of 1&#x000a0;MHz and a frequency spacing of 730&#x000a0;MHz between modes. (<bold>a</bold>) illustrates the path difference ranging from 0 to 500&#x000a0;m, while the (<bold>b</bold>) presents the path difference from 0 to 1&#x000a0;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig7_HTML" id="MO7"/></fig></p><p id="Par55">Furthermore, a comparison of Figs. <xref rid="Fig6" ref-type="fig">6</xref> and <xref rid="Fig7" ref-type="fig">7</xref> indicates that for minimal path differences, the visibility of Gaussian and Lorentzian spectra does not exhibit any significant difference.</p><p id="Par56">Consequently, regardless of whether the spectral profile is Gaussian or Lorentzian for a multi-mode laser, the optimal path differences will be even integer multiples of the laser cavity length. Assuming the path difference is zero precisely at the output of the LDV device, and considering that the beam travels to the object and back, a path difference of s&#x02009;=&#x02009;2d is created for an object distance d from the device. Therefore, the optimal object distances from the device will be integer multiples of the laser cavity length:<disp-formula id="Equ43"><label>43</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1471">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$optimum\;object\;distance = n\;L_{cavity} ,\quad n = 0,1,2,3, \ldots$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ43.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ44"><label>44</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1477">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$worst\;object\;distance = k\;L_{cavity} /2,\quad k = 2n + 1$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ44.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par57">Therefore, if a multi-mode laser is utilized in an LDV set-up, and the object is placed at an inappropriate distance close to odd multiples of <inline-formula id="IEq85"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1485">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{L}}_{{{\text{cavity}}}} /2$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq85.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> (Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ44" ref-type="disp-formula">44</xref>)), the user must manually move either the object or the device. This task can be challenging and time-consuming, depending on the object&#x02019;s size and weight, as well as the device&#x02019;s weight. However, such a problem does not occur when using a single-mode laser. Despite this, because of the higher cost and lower power of single-mode lasers, multi-mode helium&#x02013;neon lasers are generally used in LDV set-ups.</p><sec id="Sec10"><title>The solution</title><p id="Par58">In this paper, an automatic solution is proposed to address the above-mentioned problem. Instead of changing the distance between the object and the device (which is the measuring arm length), the reference arm length is modified. The schematic of the setup is shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig8" ref-type="fig">8</xref>. One of the differences of this setup compared to the standard Michelson configuration used for LDV is that, with the help of motor1, the moving mirror is capable of shifting along a rail, allowing for the adjustment of the reference arm length. The purpose of the fixed mirror is to reduce the size of the device by splitting the reference beam.<fig id="Fig8"><label>Fig. 8</label><caption><p>Schematic of the experimental set-up used in this study.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig8_HTML" id="MO8"/></fig></p><p id="Par59">The operation proceeds as follows: the distance to the object is measured by a Laser Range Finder (LRF), and depending on the measured distance, the moving mirror is adjusted to change the optical path difference so that it becomes an even integer multiple of the laser cavity length. This adjustment can be made up to the length of the laser cavity. A Dichroic Mirror (DM) is used in this setup to separate the laser wavelength from the range finder.</p></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>Auto-focus</title><p id="Par60">Autofocus automatically adjusts the focal distance of the laser beam to ensure the optimal signal is obtained. This feature is especially important when working with dynamic and complex measurement environments, where manual focusing can be time-consuming and requires a skilled operator to achieve the best result. The larger the spot size on the object, the less Doppler signal is received by the optical system. If the reflection detected by the device is below a certain threshold, it can lead to drop-outs in the vibration velocity signal. It is undoubtedly preferable for the focus to be performed automatically rather than manually. To perform a fast and accurate autofocus, the distance to the object needs to be known, which is made possible by using a laser range finder in the setup of this paper. Thus, only the lens distances need to be adjusted.</p><p id="Par61">After calculating the distance to the object, the distance between the two lenses (<inline-formula id="IEq86"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1517">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{x}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq86.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) must be adjusted so that the output beam from the laser is focused on the object. Referring to Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig9" ref-type="fig">9</xref>, where lens1 is a diverging lens, lens2 is a converging lens, <inline-formula id="IEq87"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1526">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{f}}_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq87.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the focal length of the diverging lens, <inline-formula id="IEq88"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1532">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{d}}_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq88.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the distance from the converging lens to the mirror, <inline-formula id="IEq89"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1538">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{d}}_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq89.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the distance from the mirror to the object, <inline-formula id="IEq90"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1545">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{r}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq90.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the distance from the range finder to the mirror, and <inline-formula id="IEq91"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1551">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{R}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq91.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the distance from the object to the range finder, the distance <inline-formula id="IEq92"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1557">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{x}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq86.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> can be calculated using the lens equation:<disp-formula id="Equ45"><label>45</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1563">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$1/f_{2} = 1/s_{1} + 1/s_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ45.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><fig id="Fig9"><label>Fig. 9</label><caption><p>Schematic of the autofocus system used in this study.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig9_HTML" id="MO9"/></fig></p><p id="Par62">In this equation, <inline-formula id="IEq93"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1578">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{f}}_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq93.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the focal length of the converging lens, <inline-formula id="IEq94"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1584">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}}_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq94.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>&#x0200b; is the distance from the second lens to the focal point of the first lens, and <inline-formula id="IEq95"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1590">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}}2$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq95.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is the distance from the second lens to the focal point of the beams (the object). These parameters follow the following equations:<disp-formula id="Equ46"><label>46</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1596">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$s_{1} = f_{1} + x$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ46.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ47"><label>47</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1602">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$s_{2} = d_{1} + d_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ47.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ48"><label>48</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1608">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$d_{2} = R - r$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ48.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par63">By substituting Eqs. ((46) and (47)), into Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ45" ref-type="disp-formula">45</xref>), the following relation can be obtained:<disp-formula id="Equ49"><label>49</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1619">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$1/f_{2} = 1/(f_{1} + x) + 1/s_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ49.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par64">Finally, the value of <inline-formula id="IEq96"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1627">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{x}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq86.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> is extracted from Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ49" ref-type="disp-formula">49</xref>) as follows:<disp-formula id="Equ50"><label>50</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1636">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$x = f_{2} s_{2} /(s_{2} - f_{2} ) - f_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ50.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par65">It should be noted that in the above equations, <inline-formula id="IEq97"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1644">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{f}}_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq87.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and <inline-formula id="IEq98"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1650">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{f}}_{2}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq93.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> denote their magnitude (absolute value). Laser range finders themselves have a measurement error that can vary from 1&#x000a0;mm to several centimeters, which must be taken into account in the equations. If the measurement error of the range finder is denoted as <inline-formula id="IEq99"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1656">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\pm {\text{e}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq99.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>, then, based on Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ47" ref-type="disp-formula">47</xref>) and (<xref rid="Equ48" ref-type="disp-formula">48</xref>), the variable change <inline-formula id="IEq100"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1669">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{R}} \to {\text{R}} \pm {\text{e}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq100.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> results in <inline-formula id="IEq101"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1675">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{s}}_{2} \to {\text{s}}_{2} \pm {\text{e}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq101.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. From Eq.&#x000a0;(<xref rid="Equ50" ref-type="disp-formula">50</xref>), the following two equations can be derived:<disp-formula id="Equ51"><label>51</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1684">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$x^{ + } = f_{2} (s_{2} + e)/\left( {(s_{2} + e} \right) - f_{2} ) - f_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ51.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula><disp-formula id="Equ52"><label>52</label><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1690">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$x^{ - } = f_{2} (s_{2} - e)/\left( {(s_{2} - e} \right) - f_{2} ) - f_{1}$$\end{document}</tex-math><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_Equ52.gif" position="anchor"/></alternatives></disp-formula></p><p id="Par66">Now, to accurately find the focal point, the received signal intensity is used. The distance between the two lenses is varied from <inline-formula id="IEq102"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1698">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{x}}^{ - }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq102.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> to <inline-formula id="IEq103"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1704">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{x}}^{ + }$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq103.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and the distance at which the received signal intensity reaches its maximum value is considered the focal point.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec12"><title>Experimental results</title><p id="Par67">First and foremost, the device&#x02019;s performance had to be tested experimentally to determine vibration velocity. Prior to mounting the test specimen, a galvanized steel sheet in this case, onto the exciter, the laser beam was directly aimed at the exciter shaft, and a sinusoidal excitation signal of 220 Hz with an RMS voltage of 0.6 V was applied. The measurement results are displayed in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig10" ref-type="fig">10</xref>a, where the blue lines represent the Doppler signal received by the detector, and the red lines denote the calculated velocity signal using the zero-crossing method. The RMS value of the velocity signal in this configuration was found to be 1.37. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig10" ref-type="fig">10</xref>b, represents the results of a simulation conducted using Eqs. (<xref rid="Equ6" ref-type="disp-formula">6</xref>) to (<xref rid="Equ7" ref-type="disp-formula">8</xref>) for a frequency of 220 Hz and a displacement amplitude of 5&#x003bb;. In this case, the simulated RMS velocity was 1.35. A key reason for the discrepancy between the experimental and simulated RMS values lies in the mismatch between the actual vibration amplitude of the exciter and the assumed amplitude used in the simulation. Moreover, the simulation was based on an ideal sinusoidal vibration, whereas the actual excitation deviates from this idealized behavior. Nevertheless, the observed RMS deviation of 1.45% indicates a strong agreement between theoretical and experimental results. It is worth noting that the velocity signal presented here is raw and unprocessed; its quality could be further improved through filtering and smoothing techniques, although such signal processing is beyond the scope of the present study.<fig id="Fig10"><label>Fig. 10</label><caption><p>Doppler signal (blue) and velocity signal (red) for a frequency of 220&#x000a0;Hz and a displacement amplitude of 5&#x003bb;, (<bold>a</bold>) experimental result for a voltage of <inline-formula id="IEq104"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1735">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$V_{rms} = 0.6 V$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq104.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> applied to the exciter, (<bold>b</bold>) simulation result.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig10_HTML" id="MO10"/></fig></p><p id="Par68">Laser focusing is one of the initial steps in optical preparation for laser vibrometry. In the proposed setup, this process is performed using an active autofocus system assisted by a laser rangefinder. The procedure starts by measuring the object&#x02019;s distance from the system using the rangefinder. Then, Motor 2 adjusts a movable lens to achieve an initial approximate focus on the object. To further refine the focus, a scanning process is conducted around this initial focal position. The scanning range is determined by the accuracy of the rangefinder measurement. At each step, the RMS value of the Doppler signal is recorded. The position corresponding to the maximum RMS value is selected as the final focus. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig11" ref-type="fig">11</xref> illustrates the Doppler signal RMS as a function of Motor 2 rotation for an object positioned 77&#x000a0;cm from the LDV. In this figure, the 0-degree position indicates the initial focus, where the RMS Doppler signal is measured at 1.02&#x000a0;V. The final focus, attained after a 10-degree motor rotation from this position, corresponds to the peak RMS value of 1.92&#x000a0;V. For this measurement, the vibrating object was a galvanized sheet excited using a B&#x00026;K model 4809 exciter. The input frequency applied to the exciter was 220 Hz, with a voltage amplitude of <inline-formula id="IEq105"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e1751">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{V}}_{{{\text{rms}}}} = 1{\text{ V}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq105.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula> directly supplied to the specimen.<fig id="Fig11"><label>Fig. 11</label><caption><p>Doppler signal RMS as a function of Motor 2&#x02019;s rotation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig11_HTML" id="MO11"/></fig></p><p id="Par69">Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig12" ref-type="fig">12</xref>a and b illustrate the Doppler signal&#x02013;time and velocity&#x02013;time plots, respectively, corresponding to the condition in which Motor 1 is positioned at the optimal focal point for the laser beam. In this configuration, the RMS value of the Doppler signal reaches its maximum of 1.92&#x0202f;V, and the RMS velocity is measured at 9.09. No drop-outs are observed in the velocity signal, indicating stable detection. To examine the effects of defocusing on both the Doppler and, more importantly, the velocity signals, two additional motor positions representing defocused conditions are presented in Figs. <xref rid="Fig13" ref-type="fig">13</xref> and <xref rid="Fig14" ref-type="fig">14</xref>. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig13" ref-type="fig">13</xref>a and b show the Doppler signal&#x02013;time and velocity&#x02013;time plots, respectively, when Motor 1 is rotated by&#x02009;&#x02212;&#x02009;30&#x000b0; from the optimal focusing position. In this case, the Doppler signal RMS drops significantly to 0.15&#x0202f;V, and the velocity signal exhibits drop-outs, with an RMS value of 8.93. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig14" ref-type="fig">14</xref>a and b correspond to a condition where Motor 1 is rotated&#x02009;&#x02212;&#x02009;60&#x000b0; relative to the optimal focal point. Here, the Doppler signal RMS reaches its lowest value of 0.08&#x0202f;V, and the RMS velocity decreases to 8.86. This degradation is clearly reflected in the velocity signal, where numerous drop-outs make it difficult to distinguish signal from noise. Comparing these plots clearly demonstrates that increasing deviation from the optimal focus not only reduces the amplitude of the interference signal, but also introduces drop-out artifacts in the velocity signal.<fig id="Fig12"><label>Fig. 12</label><caption><p>The temporal evolution of (<bold>a</bold>) the Doppler signal, and (<bold>b</bold>) velocity for the optimal focus condition.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig12_HTML" id="MO12"/></fig><fig id="Fig13"><label>Fig. 13</label><caption><p>The temporal evolution of (<bold>a</bold>) the Doppler signal, and (<bold>b</bold>) velocity where Motor 2 has rotated -30 degrees from the initial focus.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig13_HTML" id="MO13"/></fig><fig id="Fig14"><label>Fig. 14</label><caption><p>The temporal evolution of (<bold>a</bold>) the Doppler signal and (<bold>b</bold>) velocity magnitude as a function of time for the worst focus position, where Motor 2 has rotated &#x02212;&#x02009;60 degrees from the initial focus.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig14_HTML" id="MO14"/></fig></p><p id="Par70">Another essential step in preparing for laser vibrometry is adjusting the distance between the object and the LDV, as an optimal distance is required to achieve a high-quality signal. To investigate this, a 220&#x000a0;Hz frequency was applied to the sample, similar to the previous test, and the amplitude of the DC-removed interferometric signal voltage was measured at various object distances (different optical path differences), as shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig15" ref-type="fig">15</xref>. During this test, the reference arm length remained constant.<fig id="Fig15"><label>Fig. 15</label><caption><p>Doppler signal amplitude as a function of the object&#x02019;s distance from the device, with a constant reference arm length.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig15_HTML" id="MO15"/></fig></p><p id="Par71">Analysis of this figure shows that the Doppler signal displays peak amplitudes at specific distances, with approximately 20&#x000a0;cm intervals between them, while the signal amplitude attains a minimum between two consecutive peaks. Additionally, the peak amplitude decreases as the object distance increases. This effect arises because, as the distance from the object (which, in this case, is a diffuse surface) increases, a smaller portion of the scattered beam reaches the lens aperture. Consequently, the intensity of the reflected signal the lens receives is inversely related to the object distance. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig16" ref-type="fig">16</xref>, <xref rid="Fig17" ref-type="fig">17</xref> and <xref rid="Fig18" ref-type="fig">18</xref> illustrate the time-domain interferometric signal for six different object distances: 77&#x000a0;cm, 87&#x000a0;cm, 240&#x000a0;cm, 250&#x000a0;cm, 384&#x000a0;cm, and 394&#x000a0;cm, with an applied excitation frequency of 220&#x000a0;Hz. In these figures, the left plot corresponds to an optimal distance, while the right plot represents the subsequent suboptimal distance. Table <xref rid="Tab1" ref-type="table">1</xref> presents the RMS values of the Doppler signal at the specified object-to-sensor distances.<fig id="Fig16"><label>Fig. 16</label><caption><p>Doppler signal-time plot for a distance of (<bold>a</bold>) 77&#x000a0;cm, and (<bold>b</bold>) 88&#x000a0;cm with a 220&#x000a0;Hz excitation frequency.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig16_HTML" id="MO16"/></fig><fig id="Fig17"><label>Fig. 17</label><caption><p>Doppler signal-time plot for a distance of (<bold>a</bold>) 240&#x000a0;cm, and a (<bold>b</bold>) 250&#x000a0;cm with a 220&#x000a0;Hz excitation frequency.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig17_HTML" id="MO17"/></fig><fig id="Fig18"><label>Fig. 18</label><caption><p>Doppler signal-time plot for a (<bold>a</bold>) 384&#x000a0;cm distance, and (<bold>b</bold>) a 394&#x000a0;cm distance with a 220&#x000a0;Hz excitation frequency.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig18_HTML" id="MO18"/></fig><table-wrap id="Tab1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Doppler signal amplitude at selected optimal and critical object-to-sensor distances.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td align="left">Distance (cm)</td><td align="left">77</td><td align="left">87</td><td align="left">240</td><td align="left">250</td><td align="left">384</td><td align="left">394</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Doppler signal amplitude (voltage)</td><td align="left">2.8</td><td align="left">0.31</td><td align="left">0.74</td><td align="left">0.1</td><td align="left">0.45</td><td align="left">0.07</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p><p id="Par72">A significant difference in the Doppler signal amplitude is clearly observed, despite only a 10&#x000a0;cm variation between the two adjacent measurement positions.</p><p id="Par73">Another set of measurements was conducted for various object distances. However, this time, before each measurement, the movable reference arm was adjusted using Motor 1 to ensure that the optical path difference was an even integer multiple of the cavity length. Consequently, the Doppler signal plot for different measurement distances was obtained, as illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig19" ref-type="fig">19</xref>. By carefully examining this figure, it can be observed how the automatic adjustment of the reference arm eliminates the periodic amplitude variations in the Doppler signal across different distances.<fig id="Fig19"><label>Fig. 19</label><caption><p>Doppler signal amplitude as a function of distance for the case where the reference arm length varies based on the object&#x02019;s distance from the device.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig19_HTML" id="MO19"/></fig></p><p id="Par74">The advantage of the proposed vibrometry system over conventional LDV configurations is more clearly observable in the velocity signal. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig20" ref-type="fig">20</xref> presents the temporal evolutions of velocity for a case in which the object is located at a distance of 250&#x0202f;cm and subjected to a 220&#x0202f;Hz excitation frequency. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig20" ref-type="fig">20</xref>a shows the results obtained using a traditional LDV setup, where numerous drop-outs are visible in the velocity signal. The RMS velocity in this configuration is measured at 9.07. In contrast, Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig20" ref-type="fig">20</xref>b displays the velocity signal acquired using the proposed system introduced in this study, with an RMS velocity of 9.28. The noise-free appearance of the second plot, in contrast to the noisy signal in the first, qualitatively demonstrates the superiority of the proposed LDV configuration. Additionally, an RMS difference of approximately 0.2 quantitatively highlights the performance improvement achieved by the new approach.<fig id="Fig20"><label>Fig. 20</label><caption><p>The temporal distribution of velocity measured using (<bold>a</bold>) the conventional LDV configuration and (<bold>b</bold>) the proposed LDV setup developed in this study, for a 2.5&#x0202f;m distance between the object and the device under an excitation frequency of 220&#x0202f;Hz.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig20_HTML" id="MO20"/></fig></p><p id="Par75">The final step is validating the performance of the LDV set-up, which, in this case, is achieved by comparing the frequency-domain measurement data with that from an accelerometer. As illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig21" ref-type="fig">21</xref>, at this stage, a load cell was installed at the exciter&#x02019;s head, at the point where it connects to the plate, to measure the force applied to the sample during the FRF measurement.<fig id="Fig21"><label>Fig. 21</label><caption><p>Image of the vibrating specimen alongside with the exciter.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig21_HTML" id="MO21"/></fig></p><p id="Par76">Throughout all stages, a random excitation with a frequency range of 0 to 1&#x000a0;kHz was applied to the sample by the exciter. To simplify peak visualization and facilitate comparison across plots, only the 0&#x02013;300&#x0202f;Hz frequency range is displayed. Additionally, a Gaussian smoothing filter was applied to all FRF curves. The frequency peaks in the subsequent FRF plots are marked with solid red circles. In the first stage, measurements were taken using only the laser, without attaching the accelerometer to the vibrating sample. The resulting FRF is shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig22" ref-type="fig">22</xref>.<fig id="Fig22"><label>Fig. 22</label><caption><p>FRF plot of the specimen measured by LDV.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig22_HTML" id="MO22"/></fig></p><p id="Par77">In the next stage, a B&#x00026;K accelerometer 4396 weighing 18.2 g was attached to the same location where the laser was previously irradiated, and the FRF measurement was conducted. The corresponding plot is displayed in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig23" ref-type="fig">23</xref>.<fig id="Fig23"><label>Fig. 23</label><caption><p>FRF plot of the sample, measured using the accelerometer.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig23_HTML" id="MO23"/></fig></p><p id="Par78">By merging the two plots of Figs. <xref rid="Fig22" ref-type="fig">22</xref> and <xref rid="Fig23" ref-type="fig">23</xref>, the resulting Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig24" ref-type="fig">24</xref> displays the FRF plot derived from both the laser vibrometer and the accelerometer. In this figure, the blue plot represents the LDV, while the orange plot corresponds to the accelerometer. Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig24" ref-type="fig">24</xref> presents a comparison between the FRF plot of the galvanized sheet measured by LDV and the accelerometer devices. Comparing the plots from both devices, it is evident that there is a significant difference in the natural frequencies measured by the two devices. Specifically, for frequencies below 200&#x000a0;Hz, this difference manifests as a shift in the peaks, while at higher frequencies, new peaks appear. This discrepancy can be attributed to the weight of the accelerometer, the properties of the adhesive used between the accelerometer and the specimen, or a combination of both factors.<fig id="Fig24"><label>Fig. 24</label><caption><p>Comparison of the FRF plot of the sample as measured by LDV (green) and the accelerometer (orange).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig24_HTML" id="MO24"/></fig></p><p id="Par79">Two additional tests were performed using the LDV. In both tests, while the accelerometer was mounted on the specimen, the laser beam was initially directed onto the accelerometer, as illustrated in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig25" ref-type="fig">25</xref>a, and the corresponding FRF plot for this set-up is shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig26" ref-type="fig">26</xref>. Table. <xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref> presents the frequency peak values obtained from both the proposed LDV and the accelerometer. Except for the 6.6&#x0202f;Hz peak, which is absent in the accelerometer data, the relative frequency deviation (&#x003b4;) across the remaining peaks is predominantly zero. The maximum deviation (<inline-formula id="IEq106"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e2045">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\updelta }_{{{\text{max}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq106.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) is 0.6&#x0202f;Hz, while the average deviation (<inline-formula id="IEq107"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e2051">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\updelta }_{{{\text{avg}}}}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq107.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>) is 0.16&#x0202f;Hz. These values strongly support the accuracy and reliability of the LDV system employed in this study. Subsequently, the laser was aimed at the specimen, near the accelerometer (as depicted in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig25" ref-type="fig">25</xref>b), and the corresponding FRF plot for this configuration can be found in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig27" ref-type="fig">27</xref>. To compare all cases where the accelerometer was mounted on the specimen and to evaluate the performance of the LDV, the normalized FRF plots for these three scenarios are shown in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig28" ref-type="fig">28</xref>. In this figure, the measurements taken by the LDV are depicted with the laser spot on the accelerometer in blue, on the specimen in green, while the accelerometer measurements are represented in orange. By comparing these three plots, it is evident that the FRF peaks for all three cases align, except for a few instances where the green plot exhibits a relatively noticeable difference compared to the other two. This could suggest that some internal vibrations related to the accelerometer system, including the adhesive interface, are present in measurements taken either by the accelerometer or the LDV on the accelerometer. Alternatively, this discrepancy might also be due to the slight distance between the measurement points on the specimen, which could cause a peak to appear at one point and diminish at another.<fig id="Fig25"><label>Fig. 25</label><caption><p>Image of the LDV laser spot (<bold>a</bold>) on the accelerometer, and (<bold>b</bold>) on the specimen.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig25_HTML" id="MO25"/></fig><fig id="Fig26"><label>Fig. 26</label><caption><p>FRF plot measured by LDV for the case where the accelerometer is mounted on the specimen and the laser is directed onto the accelerometer.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig26_HTML" id="MO26"/></fig><table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>FRF frequency peaks measured using the accelerometer and the LDV with laser beam focused on the accelerometer.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Accelerometer</th><th align="left"/><th align="left">20.3</th><th align="left">67.8</th><th align="left">79.1</th><th align="left">105.6</th><th align="left">110.6</th><th align="left">179.1</th><th align="left">184.4</th><th align="left">194.4</th><th align="left">198.4</th><th align="left">225.9</th><th align="left">236.6</th><th align="left">279.4</th><th align="left">281.8</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">LDV (laser on accelerometer)</td><td char="." align="char">6.6</td><td align="left">20.3</td><td align="left">67.8</td><td char="." align="char">78.8</td><td align="left">105.6</td><td align="left">110.6</td><td char="." align="char">179.4</td><td align="left">184.4</td><td char="." align="char">193.8</td><td char="." align="char">198.7</td><td align="left">225.9</td><td align="left">236.6</td><td char="." align="char">279.7</td><td char="." align="char">282.2</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><inline-formula id="IEq108"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e2178">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\delta$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq108.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula>(difference)</td><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0</td><td char="." align="char">0.3</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0</td><td char="." align="char">0.3</td><td align="left">0</td><td char="." align="char">0.6</td><td char="." align="char">0.3</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0</td><td char="." align="char">0.3</td><td char="." align="char">0.4</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><inline-formula id="IEq109"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e2215">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\delta_{max}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq109.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula></td><td char="." align="char" colspan="3">0.6</td><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left"><inline-formula id="IEq110"><alternatives><tex-math id="d33e2237">\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\delta_{avg}$$\end{document}</tex-math><inline-graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Article_IEq110.gif"/></alternatives></inline-formula></td><td char="." align="char" colspan="3">0.16</td><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td char="." align="char"/><td char="." align="char"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><fig id="Fig27"><label>Fig. 27</label><caption><p>FRF plot measured by LDV for the case where the accelerometer is mounted on the specimen, and the laser is directed onto the plate near the accelerometer.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig27_HTML" id="MO27"/></fig><fig id="Fig28"><label>Fig. 28</label><caption><p>Comparison of the FRF of the galvanized sheet measured by LDV for the case where the accelerometer is mounted on the specimen, with the laser directed onto the accelerometer (blue), the laser directed onto the specimen near the accelerometer (green), and the measurement taken by the accelerometer (orange).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="41598_2025_23402_Fig28_HTML" id="MO28"/></fig></p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec13"><title>Conclusion</title><p id="Par80">In this study, the theoretical framework of laser-based interferometry using a multi-mode laser, which employed laser Doppler vibrometry (LDV), was presented. A numerical simulation of the Doppler signal for LDV had been conducted. The methodology for extracting vibration velocity using the zero-crossing counting technique was outlined, and this procedure was also performed practically and experimentally. Two significant optical challenges in LDV systems, optimal object distance and laser focusing, were addressed. A novel set-up was proposed to mitigate these issues by dynamically adjusting the reference arm length and utilizing an active autofocus system integrated with a laser rangefinder. A key innovation of this study lies in the design of the optical setup, which dynamically adjusts the optical path difference between the reference and measurement arms based on the distance of the vibrating object&#x02014;measured using a laser rangefinder, to achieve maximum interference signal strength. This adjustment is performed entirely automatically by modifying the length of the reference arm via a stepper motor. Experimental data obtained from a conventional LDV setup confirm that Doppler signal strength exhibits not only an inverse relationship with distance but also a periodic variation, governed by the laser cavity length, an effect consistent with the theoretical framework presented in this work. In contrast, this periodic behavior is not observed in measurements taken using the proposed setup. As a result, the new configuration demonstrates superior performance, particularly at critical object-to-sensor distances, by significantly reducing noise in the velocity signal. To verify the performance of the proposed device, FRF tests were carried out under different conditions using both the device and a contact accelerometer, and the results were compared. Measurements were conducted using the laser without the accelerometer mounted on the plate, using the accelerometer alone, and with the laser directed onto both the accelerometer and the plate. A comparison of accelerometer and laser data, when the laser was directed onto the accelerometer, revealed strong agreement in the FRF results and in the identified natural frequencies from both devices, confirming the correct operation of the proposed system. Furthermore, comparison of accelerometer data with laser measurements prior to mounting the accelerometer on the specimen revealed that the added mass of the accelerometer significantly altered the natural frequencies. This observation is fully consistent with the expected behavior of LDV as a non-contact vibration measurement technique, which does not alter the dynamic properties of the specimen. Overall, the vibration tests confirm both the accuracy and the practical applicability of the proposed configuration for vibration measurement.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn><p><bold>Publisher&#x02019;s note</bold></p><p>Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></fn></fn-group><ack><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>The authors are so thankful to Professor Ali Asghar Jafari, thank you for guiding and sharing his experimental equipment.</p></ack><notes notes-type="author-contribution"><title>Author contributions</title><p>Farshid seydipour wrote paper. Fatemeh Rezaei reviewed and supervised manuscript.</p></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Data availability</title><p>All data generated or analyzed during this study are included in this published article.</p></notes><notes id="FPar1" notes-type="COI-statement"><title>Competing interests</title><p id="Par81">The authors declare no competing interests.</p></notes><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR1" publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zheng</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Pan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Huang</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An experimental study of winding vibration of a single-phase power transformer using a laser Doppler vibrometer</article-title><source>Appl. Acoust.</source><year>2015</year><volume>87</volume><fpage>30</fpage><lpage>37</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.apacoust.2014.06.012</pub-id></element-citation><mixed-citation id="mc-CR1" publication-type="journal">Zheng, J., Pan, J. &#x00026; Huang, H. An experimental study of winding vibration of a single-phase power transformer using a laser Doppler vibrometer. <italic>Appl. Acoust.</italic><bold>87</bold>, 30&#x02013;37 (2015).</mixed-citation></citation-alternatives></ref><ref id="CR2"><label>2.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR2" publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barati Sedeh</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Gourani</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Saneinejad</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Rezaei</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Helicopter main rotor angular velocity analysis by laser Doppler velocimetry: Simulation and experimental results</article-title><source>Appl. Opt.</source><year>2019</year><volume>58</volume><issue>18</issue><fpage>5009</fpage><lpage>5017</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1364/AO.58.005009</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">31503820</pub-id>
